Step 2 — Extracted the exact run commands from each README:
  DVWA:      git clone https://github.com/digininja/DVWA && cd DVWA &&
             docker compose up -d            -> http://localhost:4280
             (README shows 127.0.0.1:4280:80 port mapping; reset: docker compose down)
  Juice Shop: docker pull bkimminich/juice-shop &&
             docker run --rm -p 127.0.0.1:3000:3000 bkimminich/juice-shop
                                                 -> http://localhost:3000
             (loopback-bound in the README itself; --rm removes on stop)
  WebGoat:   docker run -it -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 -p 127.0.0.1:9090:9090
             webgoat/webgoat                 -> :8080 app, :9090 WebWolf
             (README documents -e TZ=<zone> for JWT lessons; loopback-bound)
  crAPI:     from deploy/docker/: docker compose pull &&
             docker compose -f docker-compose.yml --compatibility up -d
                                                 -> :8888 app, :8025 mailhog
             (reset: docker compose -f docker-compose.yml --compatibility down)
  DVGA:      docker pull dolevf/dvga &&
             docker run -t -p 5013:5013 -e WEB_HOST=0.0.0.0 dolevf/dvga
                                                 -> http://localhost:5013
             (README binds -p 5013:5013 = all host interfaces; lesson teaches
              the safer -p 127.0.0.1:5013:5013 override. Port :5013 confirmed
              current — resolves the old :5000-vs-:5013 gap.)

Step 3 — Container runs: NOT RUN BY US. No Docker binary on this machine
  (checked). Each command above is quoted from the project's own README as
  fetched today, not from memory or mirrors.

Step 4 — Port-conflict check: the five targets use distinct ports
  (4280, 3000, 8080/9090, 8888/8025, 5013) — all five can run simultaneously.

Step 5 — PortSwigger Academy: account signup and lab launch are reader-side
  (needs the reader's browser + account). Academy front page fetched
  in-session (HTTP 200, 80,753 chars): free, interactive labs, Burp Community
  compatible — confirmed on the primary page.

Safety notes verified in READMEs: DVWA and WebGoat both warn the container
makes the host vulnerable while running — lesson teaches run-only-while-
practicing + compose down afterward.

Step 6 — DVGA real bring-up (2026-10-10, depth-deepening pass).
  Docker route attempted first and abandoned with a full attempt log:
  - apt: no sources configured, `apt-get update` hung on a dead mirror (>5 min).
  - Docker 28.5.2 static binaries installed fine; daemon started with
    `--storage-driver=vfs --iptables=false --bridge=none` because this VM's
    kernel rejects overlay mounts ("invalid argument") and iptables rule
    appends ("missing kernel module").
  - The daemon ignores proxy env for registry pulls (TLS handshake timeout
    to registry-1.docker.io); crane 0.22.1 (proxy-aware) could not complete
    even a hello-world pull — the registry path is dead from this network.
  - Verdict: container bring-ups remain "not run by us"; the attempt log is
    the honest record, per the brief's "a gap is a valid result."
  Native run instead, from the project's own source (github.com/dolevf/
  Damn-Vulnerable-GraphQL-Application, cloned 2026-10-10):
  - Dockerfile/READMEs confirm the lesson's claims: README's run command is
    `docker run -t -p 5013:5013 -e WEB_HOST=0.0.0.0 dolevf/dvga`; port 5013;
    Dockerfile EXPOSE 5013. The source's config.py defaults WEB_HOST to
    127.0.0.1 — the README overrides it to 0.0.0.0, which is the lesson's
    teaching point, verified at the source.
  - requirements.txt installed with two substitutions (logged, not hidden):
    pinned gevent==22.10.2 fails to build on Python 3.12 (Cython
    incompatibility) -> gevent 26.9.0; pinned greenlet==2.0.1 conflicts ->
    greenlet 3.5.6. Everything else installed as pinned.
  - `python setup.py` -> "Reconstructing Database / Populating Database / done".
  Run 1 (WEB_HOST=127.0.0.1): `ss -tlnp` -> 127.0.0.1:5013 LISTEN (python,
    loopback only). `curl http://127.0.0.1:5013/` -> HTTP 200.
    GraphQL proof: POST /graphql `{"query":"{ __schema { types { name } } }"}`
    -> 200 with the type list (Query, PasteObject, UserObject, Mutations:
    CreatePaste/EditPaste/DeletePaste/UploadPaste/ImportPaste...). Introspection
    enabled, as the b-graphql lesson teaches to expect. `{ systemHealth }`
    -> "System Load: 0.98" — a real engine, not a stub.
  Run 2 (WEB_HOST=0.0.0.0 — the README's setting): `ss -tlnp` ->
    0.0.0.0:5013 LISTEN. This is the exact difference the lesson teaches:
    the README's `-e WEB_HOST=0.0.0.0` (plus `-p 5013:5013`) exposes the
    deliberately vulnerable app on every interface; the lesson's
    loopback bind (127.0.0.1) does not. Demonstrated, not just described.
  Teardown: app killed by PID; `ss` shows nothing on 5013; `pgrep -f dvga-src`
  empty; dockerd/containerd stopped; no stray listeners. Nothing left running.
  Tool versions: Python 3.12.3 (venv), pip 24.0, gevent 26.9.0, graphene 2.1.9,
  Flask 2.2.2. Loopback only. 2026-10-10.
